# SYNOPSIS

```ndb
service=uart program=/boot/bin/drv-uart-16550 arch=x86_64 post=cons restart
ioport=0x3f8 count=8
irq=4
```

```text
/srv/cons
```

# DESCRIPTION

`drv-uart-16550` drives the PC's 16550 serial port and serves it as the console, the file `cons`, posted as `/srv/cons`.
It is x86_64's console driver; drv-uart-pl011(3) is aarch64's.

It is a service, not a device devmgr(8) finds: svcd(8) starts it first of all, from the manifest in `/boot/svc/cons.ndb` shown above, and restarts it if it exits.
svcd mints its device objects from the root Resource, once, and gives each start its own handles to them: the I/O ports the `ioport=` record names (COM1's eight from 0x3f8), the interrupt the `irq=` record names (ISA line 4), and the listen end of `/srv/cons`.
It takes no arguments.
Once it has the ports, the kernel writes to the port only to report a panic; svcd, and every program started with `console`, write their messages to `/srv/cons`, and the driver's own go straight to its output queue.

At start it turns the port's interrupts off, turns the FIFOs on and clears them, with the receive interrupt at 14 bytes, and raises DTR, RTS and OUT2, which gates the interrupt on a PC.
It takes what arrived before the interrupt was its own, then serves.
It leaves the speed and the line format as the firmware set them.

The interrupt is an ISA line, edge-triggered, which the kernel never masks: on each, the driver takes every byte received and refills the transmitter until the interrupt identification register says nothing is pending, which lowers the line for the next edge.
If something is still pending after 64 rounds it comes back to it after its other work.
It asks for the transmitter-empty interrupt only while output is waiting, and sends 16 bytes each time the transmitter is empty.

## The console

The tree is one file, `cons`, mode 0666, owned by `cons`.
Offsets mean nothing: it is a stream.
Up to 128 connections are served at once, one for each program with console output.

Reads are cooked, as Plan 9's console is.
Bytes typed are echoed and gathered into a line, and a read returns at most one line, once it is ended, never part of the next; a read with nothing finished waits.
Typed bytes mean:

: return or newline
  Ends the line; the read returns it with a newline.
  A carriage return is taken as a newline.
: backspace or DEL
  Takes back the last rune typed, all of its bytes (UTF-8 is all text, ADR-0013), echoed as one character erased.
: `^U`
  Takes back the whole line.
: `^D`
  Sends the line as it is, without a newline; on an empty line, makes one read return 0, the end of the file, in its place among the lines.
: tab and printable bytes
  Added to the line and echoed.

Any other control byte, `^C` among them, is dropped.
A line is at most 255 bytes: a longer one ends at the last whole rune that fits, and the rest starts the next.
Finished input waits in 4 KiB, at most 64 lines; a line that does not fit, because the reader is far behind, is dropped whole.

Writes go out with each newline as carriage return and newline.
A write takes what fits in the 8 KiB output queue and waits only if nothing does, until the port has taken some.

